+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+=============================
+HBF Control Plane — Memory Tier Hint API
+=============================
+
+:Author: Manish KL
+:Created: 2025
+:Status: Experimental — RFC quality, not upstream
+
+Overview
+========
+
+The Heterogeneous memory control plane provides a kernel interface for AI
+inference runtimes to communicate future memory-access intent.  A runtime
+predicts which memory ranges a model will access (e.g., KV-cache blocks,
+attention tensors, embedding tables) and issues hints to the kernel so that
+pages can be placed in the appropriate memory tier before demand access.
+
+The system treats three tiers:
+
+- **Hot tier** — fast, small, near-compute (HBM, local DRAM node 0)
+- **Warm tier** — high capacity, higher latency (CXL-attached memory,
+  HBF-like flash, NUMA node 1)
+- **Cold tier** — storage (NVMe, SSD, object store; not yet controlled
+  by this API)
+
+The kernel is the orchestrator: it validates hints, queues them, selects a
+backend (NUMA migration, CXL copy, DAX kmem management), executes the
+movement asynchronously, and reports completion.
+
+Guiding principles
+==================
+
+1. **Advisory only** — hints are performance signals, never correctness
+   critical.  Ignoring, delaying, or partially executing a hint must not
+   cause application data loss or semantic error.
+
+2. **Runtime knows the future schedule** — the kernel does not predict.
+   The runtime knows which sequences are about to decode, which KV blocks
+   will be reused, and when.  The kernel executes.
+
+3. **Observability first** — every hint lifecycle event is visible via
+   tracepoints before any advanced policy is built.
+
+4. **Transport neutral** — the ioctl character device is a prototyping
+   frontend.  The core logic operates on an internal ``struct
+   mm_tier_range_hint`` and can be reached through ``madvise()``, a
+   shared-memory ring, or any future mechanism without rewriting the
+   backends.
+
+5. **No block layer** — warm memory in this design is byte-addressable
+   (or migration-managed) capacity, not a block device.  ``blk-mq``, file
+   systems, and ``struct request`` are not involved.
+
+Hint operations
+===============
+
+.. code-block:: c
+
+    enum mm_tier_hint_op {
+        MM_TIER_HINT_PREFETCH  = 1,
+        MM_TIER_HINT_PROMOTE   = 2,
+        MM_TIER_HINT_DEMOTE    = 3,
+        MM_TIER_HINT_RELEASE   = 4,
+    };
+
+``PREFETCH``
+    Make pages resident and optionally move them toward a preferred NUMA
+    node before demand access.  Evaluated strategies include
+    ``get_user_pages_fast()`` + ``migrate_pages()``,
+    ``faultin_vma_page_range()``, and ``MADV_WILLNEED``.
+
+``PROMOTE``
+    Move eligible pages from the warm tier to the hot tier (e.g., from
+    NUMA node 1 to node 0).
+
+``DEMOTE``
+    Move cold pages from the hot tier to the warm tier to free capacity.
+
+``RELEASE``
+    Cancel queued intent and allow normal reclaim or automatic tiering
+    to manage the range.  Does not discard application data.
+
+Hint lifecycle
+==============
+
+Every hint passes through these states::
+
+    SUBMITTED
+       |
+       v
+    VALIDATED ----→ REJECTED
+       |
+       v
+    ADMITTED
+       |
+       v
+    QUEUED
+       |
+       v
+    RUNNING
+       |
+       v
+    COMPLETED  (one of: FULL_SUCCESS, PARTIAL_SUCCESS,
+                          MISSED_DEADLINE, CANCELLED, BACKEND_FAILURE)
+
+The ``SUBMIT`` ioctl returns a ``request_id`` synchronously; the caller
+polls ``QUERY`` or waits for a tracepoint event to learn the outcome.
+
+Backend abstraction
+===================
+
+Backends implement ``struct hbf_backend_ops``:
+
+.. code-block:: c
+
+    struct hbf_backend_ops {
+        int (*query_range)(struct mm_struct *mm, unsigned long start,
+                           unsigned long len, int *src_nid);
+        int (*prefetch_range)(struct mm_struct *mm, unsigned long start,
+                              unsigned long len, int target_nid);
+        int (*promote_range)(struct mm_struct *mm, unsigned long start,
+                             unsigned long len, int target_nid);
+        int (*demote_range)(struct mm_struct *mm, unsigned long start,
+                            unsigned long len, int target_nid);
+        int (*release_range)(struct mm_struct *mm, unsigned long start,
+                             unsigned long len);
+        bool (*supports_target)(int nid);
+    };
+
+The NUMA backend (``hbf_numa``) is registered by default.  Future
+backends for CXL, DAX/kmem, or fabric-attached memory can be added by
+calling ``hbf_register_backend()``.
+
+Design decisions
+================
+
+**Why an internal struct first?**
+    The core implementation operates on ``struct mm_tier_range_hint`` so
+    that the ioctl ``/dev/hbfctl`` is a thin replaceable adapter.  This
+    protects against premature ABI commitment.
+
+**Why not ``madvise()`` for v1?**
+    ``madvise()`` is synchronous and per-VMA.  The hint model requires
+    asynchronous completion, multi-VMA ranges, deadline scheduling, and
+    per-request observability.  A future ``MADV_TIER_*`` extension is
+    possible once the internal machinery is proven.
+
+**Why asynchronous?**
+    Prefetch and migration take milliseconds for typical ranges.  Blocking
+    the submitter would defeat the purpose of advance hinting.
+
+Tracepoints
+===========
+
+Defined in ``mm/hbf/hbf_tracepoints.h``:
+
+====  ============================
+Event  Purpose
+====  ============================
+``hbf_hint_submit``       Hint received from userspace
+``hbf_hint_validate``     Range and permission check result
+``hbf_hint_reject``       Hint rejected (reason code)
+``hbf_hint_queue``        Hint enqueued for async execution
+``hbf_hint_start``        Backend execution begins
+``hbf_page_move``         Single page migrated between nodes
+``hbf_hint_complete``     Hint finished (full, partial, failed)
+``hbf_hint_cancel``       Hint cancelled by userspace or exit
+``hbf_demand_touch``      Page touched before hint completed
+``hbf_deadline_miss``     Execution finished after deadline
+``hbf_queue_full``        Hint rejected due to queue capacity
+====  ============================
+
+Usage::
+
+    perf list | grep hbf
+    perf trace -e hbf:hbf_hint_submit,hbf:hbf_hint_complete
+
+Debug interface
+===============
+
+``/sys/kernel/debug/hbf/`` provides:
+
+``stats``
+    Total hints, accepted, rejected, completed, failed, bytes moved.
+``requests``
+    Human-readable table of in-flight requests.
+``backends``
+    Registered backends and their capabilities.
+``failures``
+    Ring buffer of recent failures (1024 entries).
+``latency_histogram``
+    Submission-to-start and start-to-complete latency buckets.
+
+Process isolation
+=================
+
+- A hint applies only to the caller's own address space.
+- ``mm_struct`` is pinned with ``mmgrab()`` for asynchronous work.
+- Hints are cancelled on ``exec``, ``exit``, and ``munmap`` of the
+  hinted range.
+- Validation runs at submission time and again before execution to
+  detect concurrent modification.
+
+Security
+========
+
+- ``/dev/hbfctl`` is mode ``0600``, root-only by default.
+- No kernel address or cross-process information is exposed.
+
